# Project Rules

## Cursor-Native Boot Layer

This file is the thin workspace boot surface for Cursor-native rule loading.

Do not assume Codex/OpenAI sessions natively auto-load this file. In this repo,
Codex should rely first on `AGENTS.md` and project `.codex/config.toml`.

**Context budget (local LiteLLM vs Cursor provider models):**

- Cursor cannot gate rules by model. `alwaysApply: true` costs every Agent turn.
- Keep always-on tiny: this file + `framework-context-budget.mdc` +
  `framework-user-interaction-style.mdc` + `framework-ai-agent-model-lanes.mdc`.
- Full `framework-*` / Ansible / NetBox / troubleshooting rules are
  **on-demand** (`alwaysApply: false`, often with `globs`). Prefer them on
  Cursor provider models with large context; do not force them into Ornith 32k.
- Tool schemas (~26k Task/Shell/…) are a separate Cursor client tax; see
  `logs/litellm-tools-capture/` and `framework-context-budget.mdc`.

For Cursor-native workspace sessions:
- read this file and the thin always-on rules above
- load heavy framework rules / skills only when the task needs them
- treat the `framework-*` family as the framework-owned layer (on-demand)

## Active Framework Rule Family (on-demand unless noted)

Always-on:
- `framework-context-budget.mdc`
- `framework-user-interaction-style.mdc`
  (includes required closeout when skills were used without the user naming them)
- `framework-ai-agent-model-lanes.mdc`

On-demand (description / globs / `@` mention):
- `framework-agent-role-and-persona.mdc`
- `framework-partner-process.mdc`
- `framework-knowledge-and-research.mdc`
- `framework-mcp-and-tool-usage.mdc`
- `framework-troubleshooting-mode.mdc`
- `framework-github-issue-workflow.mdc`
- `framework-netbox-modeling.mdc`
- `framework-plan-governance.mdc`
- `ansible-coding-standards.mdc`
- `terraform-support.mdc`
- `900--failure-and-diagnostics.mdc`
- `02--cussorrules-mcp-briefieng-GENERATED.mdc`

## MCP Gates (when loaded)

When the MCP / knowledge rules are in context:
- use `framework-mcp-and-tool-usage.mdc` for design/planning/debug/execution gates
- use `framework-knowledge-and-research.mdc` for research authority

Do not answer from memory when an MCP tool, registered `@doc`, or active rule
can provide the repo-specific answer — **if those rules are loaded for this
session**. On local 32k lanes, prefer a skill or `@` one rule over loading all.

## Cursor Conversation Start

For Cursor-native workspace conversations, output a short:

`Rules enforced this conversation:`

Include:
- `.cursorrules`
- every **always-on** rule actually injected
- name any on-demand framework rules that were attached this turn

## Homelab Reference Library (HRL) indexes

HRL indexes are **not** auto-injected. When library truth is needed (LangGraph,
Langfuse, learn-later frameworks, SOURCE decisions), use skill
`hrl-library-index-entry` or open:

- `/Users/joshc/develop/homelab-reference-library/indexes/technologies.md`
- `/Users/joshc/develop/homelab-reference-library/indexes/tasks.md`
- `/Users/joshc/develop/homelab-reference-library/implementation-guides/langgraph/recipes.md`

Repo roots carry `context7.json` for Context7 library submission (dashboard if
API add fails — see `docs/diagnostics/context7-add-repo-api--2026-07-24.md`).

## Active Constraints For Ansible Work

When writing, reviewing, or modifying Ansible tasks, roles, or playbooks:
- role variables must use the `role_name_` prefix
- use `.yml`, not `.yaml`
- do not use `set_fact` to override role defaults or vars
- roles must include `meta/argument_specs.yml` and a README
- load `ansible-coding-standards.mdc` when doing substantial Ansible authorship
  on a Cursor provider model

## Failure Evidence Rule

When anything fails or is unreachable:
- do not guess the cause
- collect evidence first
- stop after repeated failed fix cycles and switch to documented research
- load troubleshooting/failure rules when debugging beyond a quick probe
